498
00:20:35.987 --> 00:20:38.088
Good! Wendy's it is. Yeah.

499
00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:39.488
Do you know?
I've never had a Wendy.

500
00:20:40.785 --> 00:20:42.489
(CHUCKLES)
That came out wrong.

501
00:20:42.591 --> 00:20:44.294
- (BARNES AND PAXTON CHUCKLE)
- It's okay.

502
00:20:44.396 --> 00:20:48.198
MR. REED: So when I started
studying theology.

503
00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:50.328
the last thing
I wanted to do was find...

504
00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:53.003
the Wendy's of religions.

505
00:20:53.105 --> 00:20:55.064
I was just writing
a research paper

506
00:20:55.166 --> 00:20:57.570
for a college class. and I was
content with dabbling.

507
00:20:57.673 --> 00:21:00.671
So. some McNuggets here.
BK Whopper over there.

508
00:21:00.773 --> 00:21:03.642
whatever fit the mood.
(INHALES DEEPLY)

509
00:21:03.744 --> 00:21:07.910
As I studied the genres.
McD. BK. In-N-Out.

510
00:21:08.013 --> 00:21:12.218
i.e.. Mormonism. Scientology.
Islam. Buddhism.

511
00:21:12.320 --> 00:21:14.652
as I got closer to God

512
00:21:14.754 --> 00:21:18.690
through genre
and rigorous study.

513
00:21:18.792 --> 00:21:21.299
as I worked
on my personal relationship

514
00:21:21.401 --> 00:21:24.434
with Heavenly Father.
and I think strengthened it.

515
00:21:25.399 --> 00:21:26.997
do you know what I found?

516
00:21:28.768 --> 00:21:31.974
The more you know.
the less you know.

517
00:21:32.076 --> 00:21:33.074
(CANDLE SIZZLING OUT)

518
00:21:33.176 --> 00:21:34.475
(UNSETTLING MUSIC PLAYING)

519
00:21:34.577 --> 00:21:37.008
And by the time I was 50.
I was malnourished

520
00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:40.544
from the fast food of religion
I'd been packing into my brain

521
00:21:40.646 --> 00:21:43.120
for the best part of a decade.

522
00:21:43.222 --> 00:21:46.586
Every sect. cult.
creed. denomination

523
00:21:46.688 --> 00:21:48.855
all claimed to be the one.
true doctrine.

524
00:21:48.957 --> 00:21:52.561
and yet none seemed true when
held under the microscope.

525
00:21:55.300 --> 00:21:57.467
So I wondered
what else was out there.

526
00:21:59.332 --> 00:22:01.103
I promise you.

527
00:22:01.205 --> 00:22:03.699
the last thing I wanted to do

528
00:22:03.802 --> 00:22:05.937
was find
the one true religion.

529
00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:10.810
But unfortunately. I did.

530
00:22:16.519 --> 00:22:18.884
(UNSETTLING MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

531
00:22:22.192 --> 00:22:24.694
Can we meet your wife?

532
00:22:24.796 --> 00:22:25.855
Please?

533
00:22:25.957 --> 00:22:28.496
MR. REED: Of course. yes!
(CHUCKLES)

534
00:22:28.598 --> 00:22:31.630
She just has to be present.
and we want

535
00:22:31.732 --> 00:22:34.202
- to meet her as well.
- BARNES: Yeah.

536
00:22:34.304 --> 00:22:36.374
Sorry. I'll... I'll go ask.

537
00:22:42.046 --> 00:22:43.579
(TIMER WINDING)

538
00:22:45.576 --> 00:22:47.444
(DOOR CREAKS)

539
00:22:47.546 --> 00:22:49.618
(FOOTSTEPS RECEDING)

540
00:22:54.992 --> 00:22:56.255
(DOOR OPENS)

541
00:22:56.357 --> 00:22:57.993
(LIGHT BUZZING)

542
00:22:58.095 --> 00:22:59.527
(CLOCK TICKING)

543
00:22:59.630 --> 00:23:01.960
PAXTON: Sister Barnes.
I don't... I don't know.

544
00:23:03.697 --> 00:23:05.034
Yeah.
I don't want to spook him.

545
00:23:05.136 --> 00:23:06.635
but we should wrap this.

546
00:23:07.334 --> 00:23:08.901
Any messages?

547
00:23:10.537 --> 00:23:12.974
- Nothing.
- (EXHALES)

548
00:23:13.076 --> 00:23:14.872
Okay. Um...

549
00:23:14.975 --> 00:23:17.580
let's just politely wrap this
and head back.

550
00:23:18.415 --> 00:23:19.915
PAXTON:
Just the highlights...

551
00:23:20.017 --> 00:23:22.013
(CRACKLING)

552
00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:28.987
(OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)

553
00:23:33.998 --> 00:23:36.159
(SCRAPING)

554
00:23:44.403 --> 00:23:45.839
(GASPS)

555
00:23:49.014 --> 00:23:50.943
(CLOCK TICKING)

556
00:23:53.916 --> 00:23:56.487
- (OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)
- (CLOCK TICKING INTENSIFIES)

557
00:23:58.083 --> 00:23:59.255
- (DOOR OPENS)
- (MUSIC AND TICKING STOPS)

558
00:23:59.357 --> 00:24:01.651
MR. REED:
And she is finally ready.

559
00:24:01.753 --> 00:24:03.522
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

560
00:24:03.624 --> 00:24:05.155
(CHUCKLES) Sorry about that.

561
00:24:07.094 --> 00:24:10.361
(EXHALES) Come on through.
She's eager to... meet you.

562
00:24:10.463 --> 00:24:11.733
There we are.

563
00:24:12.466 --> 00:24:14.131
- This way.
- Okay.

564
00:24:15.073 --> 00:24:17.233
(GLASSES CLINKING)

565
00:24:22.681 --> 00:24:25.381
One moment. Mr. Reed.
Uh. we're just getting a call

566
00:24:25.483 --> 00:24:27.011
from the Relief Society
president.

567
00:24:27.113 --> 00:24:28.344
- We'll be right there.
- Oh. do you need a minute?

568
00:24:28.446 --> 00:24:29.551
Hi.

569
00:24:29.653 --> 00:24:30.884
- This is Sister Barnes. Um...
- I'll wait.

570
00:24:30.986 --> 00:24:32.385
- I'll wait on the other side.
- Yeah. Just one moment.

571
00:24:32.487 --> 00:24:33.689
- Thank you.
- Yeah.

572
00:24:40.329 --> 00:24:42.225
(DOOR CREAKS. SHUTS)

573
00:24:45.735 --> 00:24:47.436
- (SOFTLY) Should we leave?
- (SOFTLY) Now?

574
00:24:47.538 --> 00:24:50.004
- (SOFTLY) Yeah?
- (SOFTLY) Yes? Yes.

575
00:24:50.106 --> 00:24:51.341
(EXHALES)

576
00:24:51.443 --> 00:24:53.544
- (FLOOR CREAKING)
- (TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)

577
00:24:57.844 --> 00:25:00.276
(TENSE MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

578
00:25:01.481 --> 00:25:02.685
What is it?

579
00:25:03.219 --> 00:25:04.447
Our bikes.

580
00:25:04.549 --> 00:25:05.821
Yeah?

581
00:25:06.290 --> 00:25:07.285
They're locked outside.

582
00:25:07.387 --> 00:25:08.592
So what?

583
00:25:09.259 --> 00:25:10.627
He has our coats.

584
00:25:11.795 --> 00:25:13.723
The key to the bike lock
is in my coat pocket.

585
00:25:13.825 --> 00:25:15.359
So we need our coats.

586
00:25:15.461 --> 00:25:17.234
Yeah.

587
00:25:17.336 --> 00:25:19.467
- We do.
- I don't know. do we?

588
00:25:19.569 --> 00:25:20.697
Walk back
without coats and bikes?

589
00:25:20.799 --> 00:25:22.567
What is that. two miles?

590
00:25:22.669 --> 00:25:24.803
No. I think it's four.

591
00:25:24.905 --> 00:25:26.375
It's a little over the top.
but I...

592
00:25:26.477 --> 00:25:28.143
I'm fine.
I'm fine to walk. yeah?

593
00:25:28.245 --> 00:25:30.647
(DOORKNOB RATTLING)

594
00:25:32.680 --> 00:25:34.413
- It's stuck.
- Unlock it.

595
00:25:34.515 --> 00:25:36.186
(TENSE MUSIC BUILDS)

596
00:25:36.288 --> 00:25:38.751
(WHISPERS) There's no... lock.

597
00:25:38.853 --> 00:25:40.319
BARNES: (WHISPERING)
What? Pull harder.

598
00:25:40.421 --> 00:25:42.157
Here. Here.

599
00:25:42.259 --> 00:25:44.225
(DOORKNOB RATTLING)

600
00:25:44.327 --> 00:25:45.389
BARNES: All right.

601
00:25:45.492 --> 00:25:47.229
(RATTLING)

602
00:25:47.331 --> 00:25:49.132
(OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)

603
00:25:49.234 --> 00:25:51.635
(PAXTON BREATHING HEAVILY)

604
00:25:52.731 --> 00:25:54.237
(BARNES GRUNTING)

605
00:25:54.339 --> 00:25:55.734
- (CREAKING)
- (DOOR CLOSES)

606
00:25:55.836 --> 00:25:58.041
- (SHUSHES)
- (MUSIC STOPS PLAYING)

607
00:25:58.143 --> 00:26:00.010
(DISTANT FOOTSTEPS)

608
00:26:00.112 --> 00:26:01.579
(PAXTON SHUDDERS)

609
00:26:07.046 --> 00:26:08.652
BARNES: (WHISPERING)
Is he still there?

610
00:26:09.721 --> 00:26:11.484
PAXTON: (WHISPERING)
Didn't we hear him leave?

611
00:26:11.586 --> 00:26:12.887
BARNES: Are you sure?

612
00:26:14.524 --> 00:26:16.023
Should we ask him for help?

613
00:26:16.125 --> 00:26:17.188
No.

614
00:26:19.263 --> 00:26:20.257
(EXHALES SHARPLY)

615
00:26:21.695 --> 00:26:22.694
Can you fit through this?

616
00:26:22.796 --> 00:26:24.330
- PAXTON: You crazy?
- (SIGHS)

617
00:26:24.432 --> 00:26:25.402
(WIND BLOWING)

618
00:26:25.504 --> 00:26:27.869
Um. we should try Sister Hall. okay?

619
00:26:29.235 --> 00:26:32.442
Or Elder Kennedy. just so
they know where we are.

620
00:26:32.544 --> 00:26:33.977
- (BARNES SIGHS HEAVILY)
- (PHONE BEEPS)

621
00:26:34.079 --> 00:26:34.873
(LINE RINGING)

622
00:26:34.975 --> 00:26:37.307
- (WIND GUSTING)
- (OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)

623
00:26:44.320 --> 00:26:45.080
(LINE BEEPING)

624
00:26:45.182 --> 00:26:47.284
(SIGHS)
It's not going through.

625
00:26:48.824 --> 00:26:50.620
It's the storm.
Keep trying. okay?

626
00:26:50.722 --> 00:26:51.890
(PHONE BEEPS)

627
00:26:51.992 --> 00:26:54.561
- (BREATH TREMBLES)
- (LINE RINGING)

628
00:26:54.664 --> 00:26:56.964
- (DOORKNOB RATTLING)
- (GRUNTS)

629
00:26:57.629 --> 00:26:59.465
(PANTING)

630
00:26:59.567 --> 00:27:01.133
(DOOR CREAKING)

631
00:27:06.573 --> 00:27:07.608
(WIND GUSTING)

632
00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:09.073
- (DOORKNOB RATTLES)
- (PANTING)

633
00:27:09.175 --> 00:27:11.374
- (TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)
- (BREATH TREMBLING)

634
00:27:16.252 --> 00:27:17.217
(EXHALES)

635
00:27:17.319 --> 00:27:18.282
Okay.

636
00:27:20.424 --> 00:27:21.890
We ask for help. hmm?

637
00:27:24.393 --> 00:27:25.491
It'll be fine.

638
00:27:25.593 --> 00:27:27.863
(OMINOUS CREAKING)

639
00:27:29.696 --> 00:27:31.531
(RAPID TICKING)

640
00:27:31.633 --> 00:27:33.429
- (LIGHTS CLICK)
- (GASPS)

641
00:27:39.606 --> 00:27:41.408
(FOOTSTEPS)

642
00:27:41.510 --> 00:27:42.506
(TIMER WINDING)

643
00:27:42.609 --> 00:27:43.813
(CRANKING)

644
00:27:49.651 --> 00:27:51.583
(FLOOR CREAKING LIGHTLY)

645
00:27:51.685 --> 00:27:53.889
(BREATH TREMBLING)

646
00:28:05.568 --> 00:28:07.738
(FLOOR CREAKING LIGHTLY)

647
00:28:10.538 --> 00:28:11.571
(FLOOR CREAKING)

648
00:28:11.673 --> 00:28:13.744
(BOTH BREATHING HEAVILY)

649
00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:18.848
PAXTON: Mr. Reed?

650
00:28:36.962 --> 00:28:39.132
(TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)

651
00:28:44.804 --> 00:28:46.209
(MUSIC FADES OUT)

652
00:28:46.311 --> 00:28:48.405
(SIGHS. CHUCKLES)

653
00:28:55.012 --> 00:28:56.415
(DOOR LOCK CLICKS)

654
00:28:56.517 --> 00:28:58.987
<i>(JUST LIKE A BUTTERFLY THAT'S
CAUGHT IN THE RAIN</i> PLAYING)

655
00:29:00.291 --> 00:29:02.386
(DOOR CREAKS)

656
00:29:19.038 --> 00:29:20.877
And there they are! Welcome.

657
00:29:22.244 --> 00:29:25.076
Mr. Reed. you have been
too generous with your time.

658
00:29:25.178 --> 00:29:26.943
Sister Paxton and I
were just saying

659
00:29:27.045 --> 00:29:28.346
what a pleasure it's been.

660
00:29:28.448 --> 00:29:31.115
Um. but we just got off
the phone with Sister Hall.

661
00:29:31.217 --> 00:29:33.449
and she needs us
to return to the church.

662
00:29:33.551 --> 00:29:36.593
- MR. REED: Oh! Oh. well...
- Unfortunately. yeah.

663
00:29:36.695 --> 00:29:39.560
I won't keep you
if you wish to leave.

664
00:29:39.662 --> 00:29:41.997
We've been summoned back.

665
00:29:42.497 --> 00:29:44.128
Yeah.

666
00:29:44.230 --> 00:29:45.633
Damn it! Damn it.
'cause we were just

667
00:29:45.735 --> 00:29:46.936
getting started. weren't we?

668
00:29:47.038 --> 00:29:49.002
You know. you had me thinking
that maybe you were

669
00:29:49.104 --> 00:29:51.773
onto something
with your visit. (CHUCKLES)

670
00:29:51.875 --> 00:29:54.944
I've been impressed.
but I understand.

671
00:29:55.844 --> 00:29:57.008
Uh. yeah. your coats are here.

672
00:29:57.110 --> 00:29:58.579
hanging on the back
of the pew.

673
00:29:58.681 --> 00:30:00.512
Excuse me one moment.

674
00:30:00.614 --> 00:30:02.019
Yeah.

675
00:30:02.121 --> 00:30:03.513
<i>(JUST LIKE A BUTTERFLY THAT'S
CAUGHT IN THE RAIN</i> PLAYING)

676
00:30:15.266 --> 00:30:16.361
(THUD)

677
00:30:19.569 --> 00:30:21.597
(WATER SPLASHING)

678
00:30:33.682 --> 00:30:35.017
(MOUTHING) Key.

679
00:30:52.771 --> 00:30:55.371
So... so we just. uh.
need your help

680
00:30:55.473 --> 00:30:56.605
with the door. Um...

681
00:30:56.707 --> 00:30:58.636
It's... it's a little tricky.

682
00:30:58.739 --> 00:30:59.976
I'm sorry?

683
00:31:00.078 --> 00:31:02.474
PAXTON: I think your door is
a bit stuck out there.

684
00:31:02.909 --> 00:31:04.178
The door won't open.

685
00:31:05.144 --> 00:31:06.375
PAXTON:
The front door.

686
00:31:06.477 --> 00:31:08.314
Yeah. the front door
won't open again.

687
00:31:09.180 --> 00:31:10.921
It opened when we came in.

688
00:31:11.023 --> 00:31:12.722
- (CHUCKLES NERVOUSLY)
- MR. REED: Yeah. (CHUCKLES)

689
00:31:12.824 --> 00:31:14.485
Maybe if you unlock it.
it will open?

690
00:31:14.587 --> 00:31:16.687
No. no. I... I... I understand
what you're asking me.

691
00:31:16.789 --> 00:31:18.259
but. uh.

692
00:31:18.828 --> 00:31:21.028
the deadbolts are on a timer.

693
00:31:21.130 --> 00:31:23.294
I got carried away
with our conversation.

694
00:31:23.396 --> 00:31:25.197
I didn't realize the brace
had been set.

695
00:31:25.300 --> 00:31:27.263
I should have pulled the pin
when you came in.

696
00:31:27.365 --> 00:31:29.869
but I forgot.
So if you are now

697
00:31:29.971 --> 00:31:31.942
regrettably ready to leave.

698
00:31:32.044 --> 00:31:33.911
you'll have to exit
through the back of my house.

699
00:31:35.875 --> 00:31:37.545
Uh. where?

700
00:31:37.647 --> 00:31:38.982
Just through here.

701
00:31:41.184 --> 00:31:43.817
Well. can you just unlock
the front one. please?

702
00:31:43.919 --> 00:31:45.853
We would like to go that way.

703
00:31:45.955 --> 00:31:47.954
Just so we don't
get turned around

704
00:31:48.056 --> 00:31:50.287
and confused
when we get outside.

705
00:31:50.389 --> 00:31:53.590
Yeah. it won't open again
'til morning.

706
00:31:56.799 --> 00:31:58.664
(MUSIC STOPS PLAYING)

707
00:31:58.766 --> 00:32:00.404
Please. sir?

708
00:32:00.506 --> 00:32:01.365
MR. REED: Oh. I'm sorry.

709
00:32:01.467 --> 00:32:04.239
I promise you.
it's safe to leave this way.

710
00:32:04.341 --> 00:32:05.404
Huh?

711
00:32:09.144 --> 00:32:12.043
It's a little unusual
to have a door

712
00:32:12.145 --> 00:32:15.451
that. um. locks on a timer?

713
00:32:15.553 --> 00:32:16.547
MR. REED: Uh...

714
00:32:17.589 --> 00:32:18.651
Yes. well. I see that.

715
00:32:18.753 --> 00:32:20.587
(CHUCKLES) Yeah.

716
00:32:20.689 --> 00:32:21.783
And it's...

717
00:32:21.885 --> 00:32:24.793
Well. it's a little unusual
that it can't be unlocked.

718
00:32:24.895 --> 00:32:26.260
MR. REED: No.
I... I... I know.

719
00:32:26.362 --> 00:32:30.097
I am self-aware enough to know
how this might look.

720
00:32:30.199 --> 00:32:32.901
Older man. two young women
in his house.

721
00:32:33.003 --> 00:32:35.638
but I assure you.
you are welcome to leave

722
00:32:35.740 --> 00:32:36.839
through... through the back.

723
00:32:36.941 --> 00:32:38.268
You saw my house
from the outside. right?

724
00:32:38.370 --> 00:32:39.369
BARNES: Mm-hmm.
MR. REED: Right!

725
00:32:39.471 --> 00:32:40.469
So. you know
it's a tiny footprint.

726
00:32:40.571 --> 00:32:42.412
The back of the house
is just there.

727
00:32:42.514 --> 00:32:43.574
(INHALES) Yeah.

728
00:32:45.547 --> 00:32:49.285
Anyway. I will leave you space
to make your decision freely.

729
00:32:49.387 --> 00:32:50.350
Yeah.

730
00:32:59.895 --> 00:33:02.899
To clarify. I am trying...

731
00:33:03.002 --> 00:33:05.534
have been trying very hard
to make it a point

732
00:33:05.636 --> 00:33:06.396
not to pressure you.

733
00:33:06.498 --> 00:33:08.103
so you shouldn't even
feel the need

734
00:33:08.205 --> 00:33:11.370
to fabricate a story
about someone from your ward

735
00:33:11.472 --> 00:33:14.202
calling you to leave.
It's... perfectly fine.

736
00:33:14.304 --> 00:33:15.978
Leave when you want.

737
00:33:16.080 --> 00:33:19.279
It wasn't an excuse.
they do need us back.

738
00:33:19.381 --> 00:33:21.178
No. I know. but you're saying
you took a call

739
00:33:21.280 --> 00:33:22.485
with someone from your ward.

740
00:33:22.587 --> 00:33:24.251
Yeah. a moment ago.

741
00:33:24.353 --> 00:33:26.021
But when you came inside.
I asked if you were okay

742
00:33:26.123 --> 00:33:28.019
with the metal in the walls
and ceilings.

743
00:33:28.121 --> 00:33:29.756
(OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)

744
00:33:29.858 --> 00:33:31.688
What do you mean?

745
00:33:31.790 --> 00:33:34.326
I mean that the metal
obstructs cell phone signals.

746
00:33:34.428 --> 00:33:36.798
so I do know
you didn't take a call. But...

747
00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:38.931
(CHUCKLES) Just to be clear.

748
00:33:39.033 --> 00:33:40.301
so you're comfortable.

749
00:33:40.403 --> 00:33:43.601
it is totally fine for you
to leave on your own accord.

750
00:33:43.703 --> 00:33:46.536
You never need to feel like
you have to... (SIGHS)

751
00:33:46.638 --> 00:33:49.305
...make excuses or...
or tell me little...

752
00:33:49.407 --> 00:33:51.279
little... little white lies.

753
00:33:52.915 --> 00:33:54.645
(TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)

754
00:33:58.384 --> 00:34:02.557
So which door
takes us outside?

755
00:34:02.659 --> 00:34:03.993
MR. REED: Pff.

756
00:34:04.095 --> 00:34:06.027
Well... (CHUCKLES)
do you have a preference?

757
00:34:06.129 --> 00:34:09.063
Wh... why would I
have a preference?

758
00:34:09.165 --> 00:34:11.700
PAXTON: Should we have
a preference?

759
00:34:11.802 --> 00:34:13.433
That is a fair question.

760
00:34:15.503 --> 00:34:17.366
Not to be completely weird.
but can your wife

761
00:34:17.468 --> 00:34:20.208
please just step in the room.

762
00:34:20.310 --> 00:34:22.305
say hello and walk us
to the back of the house?

763
00:34:22.407 --> 00:34:23.844
I'm not trying to be silly.

764
00:34:23.946 --> 00:34:25.545
I promise. I'm really not.

765
00:34:25.647 --> 00:34:29.482
but there are rules we follow.
and we were upfront with them.

766
00:34:30.918 --> 00:34:32.188
I will ask.

767
00:34:40.492 --> 00:34:43.063
Can I ask you
a question first?

768
00:34:43.165 --> 00:34:45.333
(TENSE MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

769
00:34:47.197 --> 00:34:48.732
Do you...

770
00:34:50.736 --> 00:34:53.572
still believe that my wife
is in the next room?

771
00:34:54.705 --> 00:34:57.580
Despite all the evidence
to the contrary.

772
00:34:57.682 --> 00:34:59.081
the scented candle.

773
00:34:59.183 --> 00:35:02.350
the absence of an oven
with blueberry pie?

774
00:35:03.318 --> 00:35:06.618
Or have you been politely
indulging a lie?

775
00:35:06.720 --> 00:35:08.992
If you still believe that
she's in there. I'll go ask.

776
00:35:09.094 --> 00:35:11.155
but it's something
I want you to think about

777
00:35:11.257 --> 00:35:12.454
and maybe think about

778
00:35:12.557 --> 00:35:14.959
in the context
of your beliefs.

779
00:35:15.061 --> 00:35:16.293
(FOOTSTEPS)

780
00:35:16.395 --> 00:35:18.132
Do you believe in God because

781
00:35:18.234 --> 00:35:20.629
somebody told you
at an impressionable age

782
00:35:20.731 --> 00:35:23.432
that God is real.
despite having doubts

783
00:35:23.534 --> 00:35:25.801
as you got older. despite...

784
00:35:25.903 --> 00:35:28.109
seeing evidence to
the contrary your whole lives?

785
00:35:28.211 --> 00:35:29.805
When your father
lost control of his body.

786
00:35:29.907 --> 00:35:33.444
did you think it was
God's plan to ruin his life?

787
00:35:33.546 --> 00:35:35.151
Or did you go on
believing something

788
00:35:35.253 --> 00:35:37.846
that you know is not true
just to give you comfort

789
00:35:37.948 --> 00:35:39.787
because you were afraid
of what it might mean

790
00:35:39.889 --> 00:35:41.785
if it was all a lie?

791
00:35:43.655 --> 00:35:45.993
I put the scented candle
on the table

792
00:35:46.095 --> 00:35:47.995
because I wanted you to think

793
00:35:48.098 --> 00:35:49.297
about the things
that you believe

794
00:35:49.399 --> 00:35:52.664
just because somebody
asked you to believe them.

795
00:35:57.509 --> 00:35:58.505
Can...

796
00:36:00.141 --> 00:36:01.442
Can we use your phone?

797
00:36:01.975 --> 00:36:03.180
Please?

798
00:36:04.179 --> 00:36:06.249
I... I don't have a telephone.

799
00:36:15.226 --> 00:36:19.290
You have a really
beautiful home. Mr. Reed.

800
00:36:19.392 --> 00:36:21.357
It's like a church in here.

801
00:36:21.459 --> 00:36:22.760
Did you build it yourself?

802
00:36:23.900 --> 00:36:24.994
My wife built it.

803
00:36:25.496 --> 00:36:27.330
- (WATER POURING)
- (CLACK)

804
00:36:27.432 --> 00:36:29.602
(TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)

805
00:36:34.207 --> 00:36:36.010
We're gonna leave

806
00:36:36.113 --> 00:36:37.977
through the back
of your house.

807
00:36:38.079 --> 00:36:39.077
Now. is that okay?

808
00:36:39.179 --> 00:36:40.349
MR. REED: Of course.

809
00:36:42.384 --> 00:36:43.412
BARNES: Just like you said.
it's okay.

810
00:36:43.514 --> 00:36:46.415
and we can leave. right?

811
00:36:46.517 --> 00:36:47.819
MR. REED: That's right.

812
00:36:48.254 --> 00:36:49.886
(TENSE MUSIC BUILDS)

813
00:37:04.776 --> 00:37:06.643
(DOOR CREAKING)

814
00:37:06.745 --> 00:37:08.872
(MUSIC FADES OUT)

815
00:37:11.848 --> 00:37:12.881
What is it?

816
00:37:12.983 --> 00:37:14.746
(OMINOUS MUSIC RISES)

817
00:37:21.653 --> 00:37:23.191
- (DOOR CLOSES)
- (MUSIC FADES OUT)

818
00:37:23.293 --> 00:37:25.757
- (BREATHING HEAVILY)
- Sister Barnes?

819
00:37:34.839 --> 00:37:36.867
(DOOR CREAKING)

820
00:37:40.845 --> 00:37:42.042
That's a basement.

821
00:37:42.144 --> 00:37:44.347
(OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)

822
00:37:45.447 --> 00:37:48.043
It doesn't look like
this goes outside.

823
00:37:53.351 --> 00:37:55.523
(WIND GUSTING)

824
00:37:59.163 --> 00:38:01.056
(SLOW. SUSPENSEFUL MUSIC
PLAYING)

825
00:38:10.368 --> 00:38:12.540
(WIND GUSTING)

826
00:38:17.179 --> 00:38:19.448
(TREE BRANCHES CREAKING)

827
00:38:20.346 --> 00:38:22.319
(BUCKET RATTLING)

828
00:38:23.149 --> 00:38:24.347
(SIGHS)

829
00:38:24.449 --> 00:38:26.422
(WATER RUNNING)

830
00:38:32.129 --> 00:38:34.463
(SUSPENSEFUL MUSIC PLAYING)

831
00:38:40.134 --> 00:38:41.833
(WIND HOWLING)

832
00:38:42.968 --> 00:38:47.035
I think that you can
tell that we're regrettably

833
00:38:47.137 --> 00:38:49.876
uncomfortable with this
situation here tonight.

834
00:38:49.978 --> 00:38:51.346
(CLICK)

835
00:38:51.449 --> 00:38:54.150
I'm sorry. but didn't you just
say a moment ago

836
00:38:54.252 --> 00:38:56.681
that you saw the outside
of my house?

837
00:38:56.783 --> 00:38:58.881
- So then you clearly saw...
- (DOOR CREAKING)

838
00:38:58.984 --> 00:39:01.653
...that the back of my house
overhangs a hill.

839
00:39:01.756 --> 00:39:04.792
so you would know
that you have to go down...

840
00:39:04.895 --> 00:39:06.489
(SHUDDERS)

841
00:39:06.591 --> 00:39:08.225
...in order to go out.

842
00:39:08.327 --> 00:39:09.391
(CHUCKLING)

843
00:39:09.493 --> 00:39:10.595
(DOOR CLOSES)

844
00:39:10.697 --> 00:39:12.432
BARNES: I think
it would be best for you

845
00:39:12.534 --> 00:39:15.101
and for all of us
if you could help us

846
00:39:15.203 --> 00:39:17.339
go home because

847
00:39:17.441 --> 00:39:19.603
the Stake President.
he knows where we are.

848
00:39:19.705 --> 00:39:21.208
And the Ward
Missionaries know.

849
00:39:21.310 --> 00:39:25.107
And they'll be expecting us.

850
00:39:25.209 --> 00:39:27.745
And there were witnesses.
weren't there. Sister Barnes?

851
00:39:27.847 --> 00:39:29.111
On our way here.
we passed a police officer?

852
00:39:29.213 --> 00:39:31.387
MR. REED: Whoa! The police?
(CHUCKLES) I promise you.

853
00:39:31.489 --> 00:39:34.251
you're getting way. way. way.
way too worked up.

854
00:39:34.353 --> 00:39:35.585
BARNES: Right.

855
00:39:35.687 --> 00:39:37.026
But I think that we're scared

856
00:39:37.129 --> 00:39:39.929
because you lied
to us earlier.

857
00:39:40.031 --> 00:39:41.128
Do you want me to help?

858
00:39:41.230 --> 00:39:43.400
(TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)

859
00:39:44.401 --> 00:39:45.399
You asked me earlier
if you should

860
00:39:45.501 --> 00:39:46.896
have a preference
between the doors.

861
00:39:46.998 --> 00:39:49.337
I think you should.
I think it's something

862
00:39:49.439 --> 00:39:50.833
you should consider
very carefully.

863
00:39:50.935 --> 00:39:53.207
and then make
the right decision.

864
00:39:53.309 --> 00:39:55.577
Do you want me to help you?

865
00:39:59.378 --> 00:40:00.476
BARNES: Yes.

866
00:40:00.578 --> 00:40:01.785
MR. REED: Okay.

867
00:40:01.887 --> 00:40:02.914
Then. please...

868
00:40:04.652 --> 00:40:06.687
come and stand here.
Both of you come.

869
00:40:08.255 --> 00:40:09.450
Just here.

870
00:40:09.553 --> 00:40:10.790
That's it.

871
00:40:11.857 --> 00:40:13.928
- It's a simple choice.
- (DOOR CREAKING)

872
00:40:14.030 --> 00:40:15.025
(DOOR CLOSES)

873
00:40:15.992 --> 00:40:18.398
But it should not
be made simply.

874
00:40:18.500 --> 00:40:20.602
(TENSE MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

875
00:40:21.537 --> 00:40:23.503
I need you to reflect
very. very deeply

876
00:40:23.605 --> 00:40:26.337
and then make
a sincere decision.

877
00:40:26.439 --> 00:40:28.577
(TENSE MUSIC BUILDS)

878
00:40:33.183 --> 00:40:34.484
(MUSIC STOPS PLAYING)

879
00:40:36.086 --> 00:40:36.846
Have you ever played

880
00:40:36.948 --> 00:40:39.489
the Parker Brothers game.
<i>Monopoly?</i>

881
00:40:41.857 --> 00:40:45.756
<i>Monopoly</i> is currently
published in 47 languages.

882
00:40:45.858 --> 00:40:49.631
They sell it
in over 114 countries.

883
00:40:51.000 --> 00:40:52.700
(STATIC)

884
00:40:53.802 --> 00:40:56.165
<i>(THE AIR THAT I BREATHE</i>
BY THE HOLLIES PLAYING)

885
00:41:07.647 --> 00:41:11.983
<i>♪ If I could make a wish ♪</i>

886
00:41:12.085 --> 00:41:15.052
They say over a billion people
have played Monopoly.

887
00:41:15.154 --> 00:41:18.324
But I'm guessing that only
a fraction of that billion

888
00:41:18.426 --> 00:41:19.694
have actually finished it.

889
00:41:20.395 --> 00:41:23.031
Maybe that reminds you

890
00:41:23.133 --> 00:41:24.428
of something else?

891
00:41:24.530 --> 00:41:26.702
Just a thought.

892
00:41:26.804 --> 00:41:30.601
This is what most people mean
when they hear <i>"Monopoly."</i>

893
00:41:30.703 --> 00:41:32.740
There's the pewter avatars.

894
00:41:32.842 --> 00:41:35.712
Everyone has their favorite.
I shall not tell you mine.

895
00:41:35.814 --> 00:41:36.743
Meow!

896
00:41:36.845 --> 00:41:39.708
There's the pastel money
in various denominations.

897
00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:42.351
and. of course. the jail.

898
00:41:42.453 --> 00:41:45.752
Now. this next part
is very important.

899
00:41:45.854 --> 00:41:49.652
Have you ever played
the 1904 board game called

900
00:41:49.754 --> 00:41:52.527
<i>The Landlord's Game?</i>

901
00:41:52.629 --> 00:41:55.629
It is very nearly identical
to <i>Monopoly</i>

902
00:41:55.731 --> 00:41:57.726
in every fundamental way.
There's the jail.

903
00:41:57.828 --> 00:41:59.567
There's free parking.

904
00:41:59.669 --> 00:42:01.162
There's fines and fees.
There's penalties.

905
00:42:01.264 --> 00:42:02.735
for landing
on other people's properties.

906
00:42:02.838 --> 00:42:04.736
and the ultimate goal
of forming monopolies

907
00:42:04.838 --> 00:42:07.607
to force your opponent
out of the game.

908
00:42:07.709 --> 00:42:10.074
What do games have to do
with us leaving?

909
00:42:11.477 --> 00:42:12.681
Everything.

910
00:42:13.715 --> 00:42:16.384
<i>The Landlord's Game</i>
was designed by

911
00:42:16.486 --> 00:42:18.286
American feminist
Elizabeth Magie.

912
00:42:18.388 --> 00:42:19.982
almost three decades before

913
00:42:20.084 --> 00:42:22.425
a heater salesman
from Philadelphia.

914
00:42:22.527 --> 00:42:24.788
Sister Barnes.
changed the name to Monopoly

915
00:42:24.890 --> 00:42:27.229
and sold the concept
as his own

916
00:42:27.331 --> 00:42:28.892
to Parker Brothers in 1935.

917
00:42:28.994 --> 00:42:30.325
This chap was called
Charles Darrow.

918
00:42:30.427 --> 00:42:32.735
went on to become the first
millionaire games designer

919
00:42:32.837 --> 00:42:35.230
in the history of the world.
while poor old Elizabeth Magie

920
00:42:35.332 --> 00:42:37.503
died without ever
being credited for the impact

921
00:42:37.605 --> 00:42:40.009
she had on American culture.

922
00:42:40.111 --> 00:42:41.336
(RECORD SCRATCHES)

923
00:42:42.711 --> 00:42:44.706
<i>(THE AIR THAT I BREATHE</i>
FADES OUT)

924
00:42:44.808 --> 00:42:46.616
I'm talking to you

925
00:42:47.514 --> 00:42:48.882
about iterations.

926
00:42:50.985 --> 00:42:52.949
I need you to have a very
basic understanding

927
00:42:53.051 --> 00:42:55.951
of iterating.
because I'm going to make

928
00:42:56.053 --> 00:42:57.588
a very disturbing
claim tonight.

929
00:42:57.690 --> 00:42:59.596
(TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)

930
00:43:00.829 --> 00:43:03.661
It will make your stomachs
sink a little

931
00:43:03.763 --> 00:43:06.135
and your hearts beat faster.

932
00:43:06.237 --> 00:43:09.569
It will make you sick.

933
00:43:09.671 --> 00:43:11.504
It may even...
I'm very sorry...

934
00:43:11.607 --> 00:43:13.874
make you want to...
to die.

935
00:43:17.411 --> 00:43:19.748
(FOOTSTEPS)

936
00:43:27.552 --> 00:43:28.723
(STATIC)

937
00:43:28.825 --> 00:43:30.627
<i>(THE AIR THAT I BREATHE</i>
BY THE HOLLIES PLAYING)

938
00:43:41.098 --> 00:43:42.870
Have you heard
this song before?

939
00:43:44.505 --> 00:43:46.406
You just played it.

940
00:43:46.508 --> 00:43:49.011
No. I mean before tonight.

941
00:43:49.113 --> 00:43:50.075
PAXTON: Mmm-mmm.

942
00:43:50.609 --> 00:43:51.746
I disagree!

943
00:43:51.848 --> 00:43:53.711
I think you've heard it
many. many times before.

944
00:43:53.813 --> 00:43:55.979
but we will come back to that.

945
00:43:56.081 --> 00:43:59.048
You are. are you not. monotheistic?

946
00:43:59.150 --> 00:44:01.420
We believe in Heavenly Father. yes.

947
00:44:01.522 --> 00:44:02.625
MR. REED: Okay.

948
00:44:02.727 --> 00:44:05.493
There are three major
monotheistic religions.

949
00:44:05.595 --> 00:44:09.562
Judaism. Christianity
and Islam.

950
00:44:09.664 --> 00:44:11.833
I call them. "The Big Three."

951
00:44:13.467 --> 00:44:15.468
Judaism. i.e.

952
00:44:15.570 --> 00:44:17.200
"The Original Edition."

953
00:44:17.302 --> 00:44:20.572
Christianity. i.e.
"The Most Popular Edition."

954
00:44:20.674 --> 00:44:24.077
and Islam. "The Newer.

955
00:44:24.613 --> 00:44:26.408
"Second Most Popular Edition."

956
00:44:27.783 --> 00:44:30.412
May I see your Book of Mormon. please?

957
00:44:31.816 --> 00:44:33.019
I'll give it back.

958
00:44:34.752 --> 00:44:35.755
Thank you.

959
00:44:35.857 --> 00:44:40.158
And finally. after 800 years.
this!

960
00:44:40.792 --> 00:44:43.898
Mormonism. i.e.

961
00:44:44.899 --> 00:44:47.264
"The zany regional
spin-off edition."

962
00:44:52.574 --> 00:44:55.542
These are all iterations

963
00:44:55.644 --> 00:44:57.375
of the same source material.

964
00:44:58.178 --> 00:45:01.878
These texts share
many of the same characters

965
00:45:01.980 --> 00:45:03.245
and histories albeit presented

966
00:45:03.347 --> 00:45:05.381
with different meanings
and perspectives.

967
00:45:06.090 --> 00:45:07.989
So no.

968
00:45:08.091 --> 00:45:10.086
I will not accept... (CHUCKLES)
...that you stand there

969
00:45:10.188 --> 00:45:11.594
and tell me
that you've never heard

970
00:45:11.696 --> 00:45:13.422
<i>The Air That I Breathe</i>
by The Hollies.

971
00:45:13.524 --> 00:45:17.129
when I know that you have
heard <i>Creep</i> by Radiohead.

972
00:45:18.403 --> 00:45:20.535
Oh. yes. you have. Come on.

973
00:45:20.637 --> 00:45:22.972
(SINGING)
<i>♪ But I'm a creep! ♪</i>

974
00:45:23.074 --> 00:45:24.206
Yeah?

975
00:45:24.308 --> 00:45:25.568
<i>♪ I'm a weirdo ♪</i>

976
00:45:25.671 --> 00:45:26.806
(LAUGHING)

977
00:45:26.908 --> 00:45:29.607
Okay! "What the hell
am I doing here?"

978
00:45:29.709 --> 00:45:31.176
(MR. REED LAUGHING)

979
00:45:31.278 --> 00:45:32.648
I know these are things
you are thinking right now.

980
00:45:32.751 --> 00:45:35.015
but they're also lyrics
you recognize. yes?

981
00:45:35.580 --> 00:45:36.919
Yes?

982
00:45:37.021 --> 00:45:39.686
The Hollies filed a plagiarism
lawsuit against Radiohead.

983
00:45:39.788 --> 00:45:41.054
which they later settled
by proving

984
00:45:41.156 --> 00:45:43.819
that the melody and rhythm
of <i>The Air That I Breathe</i>

985
00:45:43.921 --> 00:45:45.525
appear in <i>Creep.</i>

986
00:45:45.627 --> 00:45:46.860
How old are you? 19 or 20?

987
00:45:46.962 --> 00:45:48.464
- Something like that. Okay.
- BARNES: Hmm.

988
00:45:48.566 --> 00:45:49.961
So maybe you know
Lana Del Rey.

989
00:45:50.063 --> 00:45:51.963
who remarkably was sued
by Radiohead

990
00:45:52.065 --> 00:45:55.599
for plagiarizing <i>Creep</i>
in her 2017 song

991
00:45:55.701 --> 00:45:57.070
<i>Get Free.</i>

992
00:45:57.571 --> 00:45:58.940
Iterations.

993
00:45:59.474 --> 00:46:00.777
Over time.

994
00:46:01.610 --> 00:46:03.576
Diluting the message.

995
00:46:03.678 --> 00:46:06.816
Obscuring the original.

996
00:46:08.983 --> 00:46:14.456
Judaism is the OG
monotheistic religion.

997
00:46:14.558 --> 00:46:16.254
It should. by a wide margin.

998
00:46:16.356 --> 00:46:19.190
have the most number of
practicing members. And yet.

999
00:46:19.292 --> 00:46:22.758
it makes up only 0.2%
of the world's population.

1000
00:46:22.860 --> 00:46:24.060
Why is that?

1001
